<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Hawk Fugagli
    SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

    Permits plain HTTP, deliberately, and here is the reasoning.

    Since API 28 Android blocks cleartext by default, and this app targets 35.
    Without this file every request to an `http://` address fails before it
    leaves the phone — no socket, no timeout, nothing on the wire. Tracks is
    self-hosted, and the ordinary deployment is a box on the user's own LAN
    reached at `10.0.0.5:4080` with no certificate, so the default turns the
    normal case into an unreachable one.

    That was not a hypothetical: it presented as onboarding reporting "No Tracks
    server" for a server that was running, answering, and pingable from the same
    phone. The platform had refused the request and the app could not tell the
    difference between that and a server that was not there.

    THE TRADE, STATED PLAINLY: this permits cleartext to any host, so a user who
    types an `http://` address for a server on the public internet will send
    their password over it. Android's network security config cannot express
    "private address ranges only" — it matches explicit domains, not CIDR
    blocks — so scoping this to LAN addresses is not available.

    The mitigation is in `discoverApiBase`, which is the thing that decides
    which scheme is actually used: given an address with no scheme it probes
    **https first** and only falls back to http when TLS does not answer. So a
    deployment that supports TLS gets TLS even if the user typed neither, and
    cleartext is reached only when it is the sole thing on offer. An explicit
    `http://` is honoured without second-guessing, because someone who typed it
    meant it.

    Other self-hosted clients — Immich, Nextcloud, Home Assistant — land in the
    same place for the same reason. This is a considered position, not a default
    left unexamined.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <!--
                Both stores. `system` is the ordinary path; `user` matters
                because self-hosters routinely front Tracks with a private CA
                or a self-signed certificate, and refusing those would push
                exactly the users who bothered to set up TLS back onto
                cleartext — the opposite of what this file is trying to do.
            -->
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
